# Meta-programming and program transformations

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:clingo_elixir, path: Path.expand("../..", __DIR__)},
  {:kino, "== 0.19.0"}
])

Code.require_file(Path.expand("../support/course.exs", __DIR__))
alias ASPCourse, as: Course
alias Clingo.Control
```

Interpret reified ground programs, compare model sets, construct diverse models and translate restricted fragments toward Boolean clauses.

Run top to bottom. Worked examples check their stated answers; update those checks when editing ASP. Exercise templates are deliberately incomplete. File-based sections create their own disposable workspace; download edits before that section’s cleanup. All solving uses the Elixir wrapper.

[Course contents](../README.md) · [Concept and API index](../CONCEPTS.md).

- [Interpret grounded programs as data](#interpret-grounded-programs-as-data)
- [Build translators and diverse-model interpreters](#build-translators-and-diverse-model-interpreters)

## Interpret grounded programs as data

Exercise files: [example.lp](../assets/meta-programming/example.lp).

```elixir
workdir =
  Course.workspace(Path.expand("../assets/meta-programming", __DIR__))
```

Meta-programming separates a source program from an interpreter for its ground representation. ASPIF is a compact interchange format; reification turns that representation into ASP facts which another encoding can inspect.

Use native Ground export to compare a small program’s ASPIF and reified facts. Numeric atom and tuple identifiers are local to an export; do not rely on their particular numbering. An output directive describes what to show, not a new rule deriving its displayed atom.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "example0.lp", ~S"""
{a}.
 b :- a.
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example0.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example0.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example0.lp")])
|> IO.puts()
```

```elixir
Course.read(workdir, "example.lp") |> IO.puts()
```

### Read ground rule forms

A ground rule may use a normal literal body or a weighted body. Its head can be disjunctive, a choice, or empty. An empty disjunctive head is an integrity constraint. Use the reified rule, atom-tuple and literal-tuple facts together when reconstructing meaning.

### Reify a minimal program

Start with a single optional atom and inspect the tuple facts describing its choice head and empty body. Output filtering can hide every atom while leaving its models intact.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "example1.lp", ~S"""
{a}.
#show.
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example1.lp")])
|> IO.puts()
```

```elixir
Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example1.lp")], format: :reify)
|> IO.puts()
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "example2.lp", ~S"""
{ a }.
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example2.lp")])
|> IO.puts()
```

```elixir
Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example2.lp")], format: :reify)
|> IO.puts()
```

### Build a small interpreter

Compare the full meta encoding with the simplified version. The latter handles normal bodies and a restricted output representation. With the unmodified examples, it preserves the shown model sets of the source programs, including an empty shown set when `#show.` hides everything.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "meta.lp", ~S"""
conjunction(B) :- literal_tuple(B),
        hold(L) : literal_tuple(B, L), L > 0;
    not hold(L) : literal_tuple(B,-L), L > 0.
body(normal(B)) :- rule(_,normal(B)), conjunction(B).
body(sum(B,G))  :- rule(_,sum(B,G)),
    #sum { W,L :     hold(L), weighted_literal_tuple(B, L,W), L > 0 ;
           W,L : not hold(L), weighted_literal_tuple(B,-L,W), L > 0 } >= G.
  hold(A) : atom_tuple(H,A)   :- rule(disjunction(H),B), body(B).
{ hold(A) : atom_tuple(H,A) } :- rule(     choice(H),B), body(B).
#show.
#show T : output(T,B), conjunction(B).
""")
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "meta-simple.lp", ~S"""
conjunction(B) :- literal_tuple(B),
        hold(L) : literal_tuple(B, L), L > 0;
    not hold(L) : literal_tuple(B,-L), L > 0.
  hold(A) : atom_tuple(H,A)   :- rule(disjunction(H),normal(B)), conjunction(B).
{ hold(A) : atom_tuple(H,A) } :- rule(     choice(H),normal(B)), conjunction(B).
#show.
#show T : output(T,B), not literal_tuple(B,_).
#show T : output(T,B),     literal_tuple(B,L), hold(L).
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(
  Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example1.lp")], format: :reify),
  files: [Path.join(workdir, "meta-simple.lp")],
  models: :all,
  arguments: ["-Wnone"]
)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(
  Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example2.lp")], format: :reify),
  files: [Path.join(workdir, "meta-simple.lp")],
  models: :all,
  arguments: ["-Wnone"]
)
|> Course.display()
```

### Exercise: change the interpretation

Modify `meta-simple-ex1.lp` so that choice heads act as normal heads. For the one-atom example this should require the atom. Then change its output representation to show `true(a)` instead of `a`. These are goals for your completed encoding, not claims about the blank starter.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "meta-simple-ex1.lp", ~S"""
conjunction(B) :- literal_tuple(B),
        hold(L) : literal_tuple(B, L), L > 0;
    not hold(L) : literal_tuple(B,-L), L > 0.
  hold(A) : atom_tuple(H,A)   :- rule(disjunction(H),normal(B)), conjunction(B).
{ hold(A) : atom_tuple(H,A) } :- rule(     choice(H),normal(B)), conjunction(B).
#show.
#show T : output(T,B), not literal_tuple(B,_).
#show T : output(T,B),     literal_tuple(B,L), hold(L).
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(
  Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example2.lp")], format: :reify),
  files: [Path.join(workdir, "meta-simple-ex1.lp")],
  models: :all,
  arguments: ["-Wnone"]
)
|> Course.display()
```

### Add a dependent atom

Now add a normal rule whose body depends on the optional atom. Reification records both the rule and the conditions used by the output directives. Follow those separate roles through the interpreter.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "example3.lp", ~S"""
{a}.
 b :- a.
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example3.lp")])
|> IO.puts()
```

```elixir
Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example3.lp")], format: :reify)
|> IO.puts()
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(
  Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example3.lp")], format: :reify),
  files: [Path.join(workdir, "meta-simple.lp")],
  models: :all
)
|> Course.display()
```

### Exercise: exchange rule roles

In `meta-simple-ex2.lp`, interpret choices as normal rules and normal rules as choices. Reverse each body literal’s sign. On the two-rule example, the intended transformed program has the unique shown set `{a}`.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "meta-simple-ex2.lp", ~S"""
conjunction(B) :- literal_tuple(B),
        hold(L) : literal_tuple(B, L), L > 0;
    not hold(L) : literal_tuple(B,-L), L > 0.
  hold(A) : atom_tuple(H,A)   :- rule(disjunction(H),normal(B)), conjunction(B).
{ hold(A) : atom_tuple(H,A) } :- rule(     choice(H),normal(B)), conjunction(B).
#show.
#show T : output(T,B), not literal_tuple(B,_).
#show T : output(T,B),     literal_tuple(B,L), hold(L).
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(
  Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example3.lp")], format: :reify),
  files: [Path.join(workdir, "meta-simple-ex2.lp")],
  models: :all
)
|> Course.display()
```

### Exercise: change body conjunction

Extend the simplified interpreter so a nonempty body acts as a disjunction of its literals. Specify separately what an empty body means, then test the change on the smallest example before larger inputs.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "example4.lp", ~S"""
{a;b}.
c :- a,b.
""")
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "meta-simple-ex3.lp", ~S"""
conjunction(B) :- literal_tuple(B),
        hold(L) : literal_tuple(B, L), L > 0;
    not hold(L) : literal_tuple(B,-L), L > 0.
  hold(A) : atom_tuple(H,A)   :- rule(disjunction(H),normal(B)), conjunction(B).
{ hold(A) : atom_tuple(H,A) } :- rule(     choice(H),normal(B)), conjunction(B).
#show.
#show T : output(T,B), not literal_tuple(B,_).
#show T : output(T,B),     literal_tuple(B,L), hold(L).
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(
  Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example4.lp")], format: :reify),
  files: [Path.join(workdir, "meta-simple-ex3.lp")],
  models: :all
)
|> Course.display()
```

### Remember grounding simplifications

The next example revisits the exchanged-rule interpretation, but now a fact lets the grounder simplify other rules before reification.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "example5.lp", ~S"""
a.
b :- not a.
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(
  Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example5.lp")], format: :reify),
  files: [Path.join(workdir, "meta-simple-ex2.lp")],
  models: :all
)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example5.lp")])
|> IO.puts()
```

```elixir
Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example5.lp")], format: :reify)
|> IO.puts()
```

A rule removed during grounding is absent from the reified input. The meta encoding cannot reinterpret information that was already discarded. Its input is the simplified ground program, not the original variable-level source. Account for this boundary when designing transformations.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "example6.lp", ~S"""
{a;b;c;d}.
e :- a, 30 #sum{ 20:b; 30:c; 40:d }.
:- not e.
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example6.lp")], format: :reify)
|> IO.puts()
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "example6-reified.lp", ~S"""
atom_tuple(0).
atom_tuple(0,1).
atom_tuple(0,2).
atom_tuple(0,3).
atom_tuple(0,4).
literal_tuple(0).
rule(choice(0),normal(0)).
atom_tuple(1).
atom_tuple(1,5).
weighted_literal_tuple(0).
weighted_literal_tuple(0,2,20).
weighted_literal_tuple(0,3,30).
weighted_literal_tuple(0,4,40).
rule(disjunction(1),sum(0,30)).
atom_tuple(2).
atom_tuple(2,6).
literal_tuple(1).
literal_tuple(1,5).
literal_tuple(1,1).
rule(disjunction(2),normal(1)).
atom_tuple(3).
literal_tuple(2).
literal_tuple(2,-6).
rule(disjunction(3),normal(2)).
literal_tuple(3).
literal_tuple(3,1).
output(a,3).
literal_tuple(4).
literal_tuple(4,2).
output(b,4).
literal_tuple(5).
literal_tuple(5,3).
output(c,5).
literal_tuple(6).
literal_tuple(6,4).
output(d,6).
literal_tuple(7).
literal_tuple(7,6).
output(e,7).
""")
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "meta-ex4.lp", ~S"""
conjunction(B) :- literal_tuple(B),
        hold(L) : literal_tuple(B, L), L > 0;
    not hold(L) : literal_tuple(B,-L), L > 0.
body(normal(B)) :- rule(_,normal(B)), conjunction(B).
body(sum(B,G))  :- rule(_,sum(B,G)),
    #sum { W,L :     hold(L), weighted_literal_tuple(B, L,W), L > 0 ;
           W,L : not hold(L), weighted_literal_tuple(B,-L,W), L > 0 } >= G.
  hold(A) : atom_tuple(H,A)   :- rule(disjunction(H),B), body(B).
{ hold(A) : atom_tuple(H,A) } :- rule(     choice(H),B), body(B).
#show.
#show T : output(T,B), conjunction(B).
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(
  Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example6.lp")], format: :reify),
  files: [Path.join(workdir, "meta-ex4.lp")],
  models: :all
)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "example7.lp", ~S"""
{ a(1); a(2) }.
:- 1 { a(Item) }.
:-   { a(Item) } 1.
#show a/1.
""")
