## Build translators and diverse-model interpreters

```elixir
workdir =
  Course.workspace()

exercise_runs = false
```

Start by comparing direct Latin-square solving with solving a reified program through a meta encoding. Compare sorted shown atom sets, not enumeration order or internal atom IDs. The helper `run` performs that comparison using the NIF.

This project has two extensions: choose several weighted-diverse models, and translate general ground programs toward a restricted SAT-like representation. Learner-dependent runs remain gated by `exercise_runs`; enable them only after supplying the requested encodings.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "latin.lp", ~S"""
#const s=4.
number(1..s).
1 { latin(Item,Other,N) : number(N) } 1 :- number(Item), number(Other).
1 { latin(Item,Other,N) : number(Other) } 1 :- number(Item), number(N).
1 { latin(Item,Other,N) : number(Item) } 1 :- number(Other), number(N).
#show latin/3.
""")
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "meta.lp", ~S"""
conjunction(B) :- literal_tuple(B),
        hold(L) : literal_tuple(B, L), L > 0;
    not hold(L) : literal_tuple(B,-L), L > 0.
body(normal(B)) :- rule(_,normal(B)), conjunction(B).
body(sum(B,G))  :- rule(_,sum(B,G)),
    #sum { W,L :     hold(L), weighted_literal_tuple(B, L,W), L > 0 ;
           W,L : not hold(L), weighted_literal_tuple(B,-L,W), L > 0 } >= G.
  hold(A) : atom_tuple(H,A)   :- rule(disjunction(H),B), body(B).
{ hold(A) : atom_tuple(H,A) } :- rule(     choice(H),B), body(B).
#show.
#show T : output(T,B), conjunction(B).
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "latin.lp")], models: :all, arguments: ["-c", "s=4"])
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
Course.write(
  workdir,
  "reify.lp",
  Clingo.Ground.to_string!(
    files: [Path.join(workdir, "latin.lp")],
    arguments: ["-c", "s=4"],
    format: :reify
  )
)

Clingo.solve!(
  files: [Path.join(workdir, "reify.lp"), Path.join(workdir, "meta.lp")],
  models: :all
)
```

```elixir
run = fn program, files ->
  Clingo.solve!(program, files: Enum.map(files, &Path.join(workdir, &1)), models: :all).models
  |> Enum.map(& &1.shown)
  |> Enum.sort()
end
```

```elixir
Course.write(
  workdir,
  "reify.lp",
  Clingo.Ground.to_string!(
    files: [Path.join(workdir, "latin.lp")],
    arguments: ["-c", "s=4"],
    format: :reify
  )
)

true = run.("", ["reify.lp", "meta.lp"]) == run.("", ["latin.lp"])
```

### Weight model differences

Create `weighted-many.lp` in the workspace by copying and extending the `many.lp` starter below. `weight/2` assigns a contribution to an atom; the distance between two models sums contributions for atoms on which they disagree. Check the requested number of models and their pairwise distances independently; the optimum must respect the selected optimization ordering.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "many.lp", ~S"""
model(1..m).
conjunction(B,M) :- model(M), literal_tuple(B),
        hold(L,M) : literal_tuple(B, L), L > 0;
    not hold(L,M) : literal_tuple(B,-L), L > 0.
body(normal(B),M) :- rule(_,normal(B)), conjunction(B,M).
body(sum(B,G),M)  :- model(M), rule(_,sum(B,G)),
    #sum { W,L :     hold(L,M), weighted_literal_tuple(B, L,W), L > 0 ;
           W,L : not hold(L,M), weighted_literal_tuple(B,-L,W), L > 0 } >= G.
  hold(A,M) : atom_tuple(H,A)   :- rule(disjunction(H),B), body(B,M).
{ hold(A,M) : atom_tuple(H,A) } :- rule(     choice(H),B), body(B,M).
show(T,M) :- output(T,B), conjunction(B,M).
#show.
#show (T,M) : show(T,M).
#const k=1.
:- model(M), model(N), M<N, option = 1,
    #sum{ 1,T: show(T,M), not show(T,N) ;
          1,T: not show(T,M), show(T,N) } < k.
#maximize{ 1,T,M,N: show(T,M), not show(T,N), model(N), option = 2 }.
""")
```

> **Note.** Enable exercise_runs only after completing the required encoding. The default evaluation reports this as a pending exercise.

```elixir
if exercise_runs do
  Course.write(
    workdir,
    "reify.lp",
    Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "latin.lp")], format: :reify)
  )

  Clingo.solve!(
    files: [Path.join(workdir, "reify.lp"), Path.join(workdir, "weighted-many.lp")],
    models: :all,
    arguments: ["-c", "m=3", "-c", "option=2", "--opt-mode=optN"]
  )
else
  %{
    exercise_pending: "Complete the requested encodings, then set exercise_runs = true."
  }
end
```

### Represent Boolean clauses

A DIMACS header declares variable and clause counts. Each zero-terminated clause lists signed variable IDs. Generate Boolean choices in ASP and reject assignments that falsify a clause. The DIMACS example is executed by the native adapter, not by an external SAT process.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "example1.cnf", ~S"""
c
c some comments
c
p cnf 3 4
1 -2 0
2 -1 0
3 -1 -2 0
3  1  2 0
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.Dimacs.solve!(Course.read(workdir, "example1.cnf"), models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "example1.cnf.lp", ~S"""
{ hold(1..3) }.
:- not hold(1),     hold(2).
:- not hold(2),     hold(1).
:- not hold(3),     hold(1),     hold(2).
:- not hold(3), not hold(1), not hold(2).
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example1.cnf.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

### Translate in stages

First replace choice and weight constructs with normal rules. For tight normal inputs, express completion as choices plus integrity constraints. Finally translate the supported restricted ASPIF fragment to DIMACS. Test preservation of projected model sets at each stage; general non-tight inputs are an optional extension, not an assumption of the starter.

#### Remove choice and weight rules

Complete `meta-normal.lp` so its ground output has neither construct while preserving the source program’s answer sets. The starter is incomplete: the model-set comparison below should be false until you add the missing translations. Inspect the reified ground output as well as running model-set comparisons. Empty filtered output is evidence about the inspected constructs, not proof of full semantic equivalence.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "meta-normal.lp", ~S"""
conjunction(B) :- literal_tuple(B),
        hold(L) : literal_tuple(B, L), L > 0;
    not hold(L) : literal_tuple(B,-L), L > 0.
body(normal(B)) :- rule(_,normal(B)), conjunction(B).
  hold(A) : atom_tuple(H,A)   :- rule(disjunction(H),B), body(B).
#show.
#show T : output(T,B), conjunction(B).
""")
```

```elixir
Course.write(
  workdir,
  "reify.lp",
  Clingo.Ground.to_string!(
    files: [Path.join(workdir, "latin.lp")],
    arguments: ["-c", "s=4"],
    format: :reify
  )
)

run.("", ["reify.lp", "meta-normal.lp"]) == run.("", ["latin.lp"])
```

```elixir
Course.write(
  workdir,
  "reify.lp",
  Clingo.Ground.to_string!(
    files: [Path.join(workdir, "latin.lp")],
    arguments: ["-c", "s=4"],
    format: :reify
  )
)

Clingo.Ground.to_string!(
  files: [Path.join(workdir, "reify.lp"), Path.join(workdir, "meta-normal.lp")],
  format: :reify
)
|> String.split("\n", trim: true)
|> Enum.filter(&String.starts_with?(&1, ["weighted_literal_tuple", "rule(choice"]))
|> Enum.join("\n")
```

> **Note.** Enable exercise_runs only after completing the required encoding. The default evaluation reports this as a pending exercise.

```elixir
if exercise_runs do
  Course.write(
    workdir,
    "reify.lp",
    Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "latin.lp")], format: :reify)
  )

  run.("", ["reify.lp", "meta-normal.lp"]) == run.("", ["latin.lp"])
else
  %{
    exercise_pending: "Complete the requested encodings, then set exercise_runs = true."
  }
end
```

#### Replace a choice head

Represent the optional truth of each head atom using normal rules and suitable complementary support. Check both truth choices before applying the construction to reified tuples.

#### Replace a weighted body

The weight threshold must be represented without leaving a ground weight rule. Accumulate weighted literal contributions through ordinary rules and compare the resulting threshold behavior with the original aggregate.

### Express completion for tight programs

Complete `meta-constraints.lp` for normal programs with integrity constraints and no positive dependency cycle. Its choices generate assignments; its integrity constraints enforce the completion nogoods. The tightness restriction is essential to the stated model correspondence.

```elixir
Course.write(
  workdir,
  "reify.lp",
  Clingo.Ground.to_string!(
    files: [Path.join(workdir, "latin.lp")],
    arguments: ["-c", "s=4"],
    format: :reify
  )
)

Clingo.Ground.to_string!(
  files: [Path.join(workdir, "reify.lp"), Path.join(workdir, "meta-normal.lp")],
  format: :reify
)
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "meta-constraints.lp", ~S"""
{conjunction(B)} :- literal_tuple(B).
{     hold(|L|)} :- literal_tuple(B,L).
{     hold( A )} :- atom_tuple(H,A).
#show.
#show T : output(T,B), conjunction(B).
""")
```

Add two atom-oriented and three body-oriented constraint forms to the supplied template. Check each direction of support, not only whether true bodies imply their heads.

> **Note.** Enable exercise_runs only after completing the required encoding. The default evaluation reports this as a pending exercise.

```elixir
if exercise_runs do
  Course.write(
    workdir,
    "reify1.lp",
    Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "latin.lp")], format: :reify)
  )

  Course.write(
    workdir,
    "reify2.lp",
    Clingo.Ground.to_string!(
      files: [Path.join(workdir, "reify1.lp"), Path.join(workdir, "meta-normal.lp")],
      format: :reify
    )
  )

  run.("", ["reify2.lp", "meta-constraints.lp"]) == run.("", ["latin.lp"])
else
  %{
    exercise_pending: "Complete the requested encodings, then set exercise_runs = true."
  }
end
```

### Emit Boolean clauses

Implement the `asp2dimacs` exercise for facts, independent one-atom choices and integrity constraints. Preserve the models under the atom-ID mapping. Implement the translator in Elixir and check it with `Clingo.Dimacs.solve!/2`.

#### Handle auxiliary definitions

Grounding a conditional literal can introduce a normal rule defining an auxiliary atom followed by a constraint using it. The next example exposes that case; a translator restricted to explicit constraints must handle it deliberately.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "conditional.lp", ~S"""
{ a(1..3) }.
:- a(1), not a(Item) : Item=2..3.
{ b(1..3) }.
:- not b(1), b(Item) : Item=2..3.
#show.
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "conditional.lp")])
|> IO.puts()
```

Combine the auxiliary definition with its following constraint when the atom has no other uses. Do not leave unused auxiliary variables free in DIMACS: that would multiply the model count. Compare solution counts on your smallest translation test cases, including the examples above.

### Extend only after the restricted translation works

Possible extensions include reconstructing symbolic models from SAT assignments, combining the meta encodings, improving weight-rule translation, and supporting non-tight programs with an appropriate additional construction.

